
========================================================================
  PART 1: IS T^T = J - I - T FOR TOURNAMENTS?
========================================================================

  For a tournament matrix T:
  T[i][j] + T[j][i] = 1 for all i != j
  T[i][i] = 0

  The TRANSPOSE T^T has T^T[i][j] = T[j][i] = 1 - T[i][j] for i != j
  and T^T[i][i] = 0.

  The COMPLEMENT J - I - T has:
  (J-I-T)[i][j] = 1 - 0 - T[i][j] = 1 - T[i][j] for i != j
  (J-I-T)[i][i] = 1 - 1 - 0 = 0

  So T^T[i][j] = 1 - T[i][j] = (J-I-T)[i][j] for i != j.
  And T^T[i][i] = 0 = (J-I-T)[i][i].
  Therefore: T^T = J - I - T. YES, they are the same!

  Verification at n=3:
  All 8 tournaments: T^T = J - I - T. CONFIRMED.

  So: RSK(T^op) = RSK(T^T).
  By RSK symmetry: RSK(M^T) has shape = conjugate of RSK(M).

  BUT WAIT: This RSK symmetry holds for the CLASSICAL RSK on
  permutation matrices or biwords. For GENERALIZED RSK on 0-1 matrices,
  the statement is: if M is an n x n matrix with non-negative integer entries,
  RSK(M) has insertion shape P and recording shape Q, and
  RSK(M^T) has insertion shape Q and recording shape P.
  The SHAPE of P need not equal the shape of Q for non-permutation matrices.

  For SYMMETRIC matrices: M = M^T implies P = Q, so shape(P) = shape(Q).
  For tournaments: M != M^T but M + M^T = J - I.


